><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250709105857+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250709105900+10'00'" name="abcff29f-e3fa-4818-8569-57570d024182" page="9" coords="313.001495,369.743591,575.944336,369.743591,313.001495,358.713684,575.944336,358.713684,313.001495,358.688477,575.985535,358.688477,313.001495,347.658569,575.985535,347.658569,313.001495,347.576355,576.000244,347.576355,313.001495,336.546448,576.000244,336.546448,313.001495,336.521240,575.946228,336.521240,313.001495,325.491333,575.946228,325.491333,313.001495,325.409119,575.902954,325.409119,313.001495,314.379211,575.902954,314.379211,313.001495,314.354004,575.946167,314.354004,313.001495,303.324097,575.946167,303.324097,313.001495,303.298889,575.993164,303.298889,313.001495,292.268982,575.993164,292.268982,313.001495,292.186768,575.967590,292.186768,313.001495,281.156860,575.967590,281.156860,313.001495,281.131653,576.012024,281.131653,313.001495,270.101746,576.012024,270.101746,313.001465,270.019531,575.996887,270.019531,313.001465,258.989624,575.996887,258.989624,313.001465,258.964417,575.992920,258.964417,313.001465,247.934494,575.992920,247.934494,313.001465,247.852325,575.988770,247.852325,313.001465,236.822403,575.988770,236.822403,313.001465,236.797211,443.159851,236.797211,313.001465,225.767288,443.159851,225.767288" rect="312.656769,225.422592,576.356689,370.088287" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Although the concept of MFTC is taught in every exercise physiology&#xD;course, it remained unclear whether this information was&#xD;actually applied in sports practice. This survey uncovered that the&#xD;concept of MFTC is extensively used by high-performance experts,&#xD;who consider this information highly relevant for many different&#xD;aspects of coaching practice. Since the majority of experts are&#xD;reluctant to use muscle biopsies, numerous alternative noninvasive&#xD;estimators of MFTC are currently employed. However, not all&#xD;techniques and parameters are supported by scientific evidence,&#xD;partly because the research is not available and partly because the&#xD;negative findings of available research have not been passed on to&#xD;coaching practice. The latter offers opportunities for optimizing&#xD;experts’ education on this topic.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="9" rect="612.000000,277.743591,796.000000,369.743591"
/></highlight
